# MomentumQuest frontend environment.
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. The value below is the DEVELOPMENT one.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined into the JavaScript bundle at BUILD
# time, not read at runtime. Setting this in the production environment after
# `npm run build` changes nothing -- the localhost URL is already compiled
# into the files the browser downloads, and every API call from the deployed
# site goes to the visitor's own machine.
#
# So it has to be set for the build itself:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.momentumquest.example.edu npm run build
#
# or in the build environment of whatever runs the deploy. Use https: the JWT
# is sent on every request, and over plain http it is readable in transit.
#
# The backend must also be expecting this site: the browser sends *this site's*
# origin to the API, and the API's CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS has to list it, or the
# browser discards the response. That is the deployed frontend's own URL, not
# the value of this variable -- the two are different hosts and mixing them up
# produces a CORS failure that looks like the API being down.
#
# On Vercel
# ---------
#   Root Directory       frontend
#   Framework Preset     Next.js
#   Environment variable NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://<render-api-domain>
#
# Set it for the Production environment before the first build, and redeploy
# after any change to it -- see the note above about build-time inlining. Do not
# commit a production URL to .env.local; that file is for local development.
#
# The matching backend variables are listed in docs/deployment-runbook.md.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
